WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.760 --> 00:00:05.470
It wasn't easy for Christian Bale to prepare
for director James Mangold's Ford v Ferrari,

00:00:05.470 --> 00:00:06.770
but it was worth the effort.

00:00:06.770 --> 00:00:11.180
Looper recently spoke with Ford v Ferrari
stunt coordinator Robert Nagle about how he

00:00:11.180 --> 00:00:12.940
made the veteran actor road-ready.

00:00:12.940 --> 00:00:15.480
Here's the truth behind Bale's intense training
for the pic.

00:00:15.980 --> 00:00:20.480
To teach Bale how to drive like a real racer,
Nagle sent the former Dark Knight to the Bondurant

00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:22.810
High Performance Driving School in Arizona.

00:00:22.810 --> 00:00:26.869
For seven days, Bale spent seven hours on
the track, learning how to drive thanks to

00:00:26.869 --> 00:00:29.070
lessons from Nagle's hand-picked instructor.

00:00:29.070 --> 00:00:32.860
But that was only part of Bale's education,
and there was a reason why Boundurant was

00:00:32.860 --> 00:00:35.800
the place Bale did his first phase of Ford
v Ferrari prep.

00:00:36.020 --> 00:00:37.020
Nagle explained:

00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:41.019
"Part of the reason I picked Bondurant [was
that] Bob Bondurant was an icon from that

00:00:41.019 --> 00:00:44.030
era and was a big part of that GT40 program."

00:00:44.030 --> 00:00:48.800
Two years before Ken Miles' history-making
performance at the 1966 automobile event,

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:55.240
racecar driver Bob Bondurant completed 334
laps at the 1964 24 Hours of Le Mons.

00:00:55.240 --> 00:00:59.619
Like Miles, Bondurant was driving a car designed
by Carroll Shelby, the engineer whom Matt

00:00:59.620 --> 00:01:01.760
Damon plays in Ford v Ferrari.

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:05.780
"Just shut your mouth and let me do my thing.

00:01:08.520 --> 00:01:09.520
Alright."

00:01:09.820 --> 00:01:13.800
It was enough to win the GT class at the event,
but not the overall race.

00:01:13.810 --> 00:01:16.160
Naturally, a Ferrari came in first.

00:01:16.160 --> 00:01:20.391
For a full week, Bondurant regaled Bale and
Nagle with stories from the Ford v Ferrari

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:22.340
era of the mid-1960s.

00:01:22.340 --> 00:01:26.140
Nagle estimated that Bale and Bondurant chatted
for four or five hours a day.

00:01:26.140 --> 00:01:30.850
To Nagle's surprise, Bondurant was also close
friends with Miles, and had plenty of insights

00:01:30.850 --> 00:01:32.660
to share about the late star.

00:01:32.660 --> 00:01:33.660
Nagle dished:

00:01:33.900 --> 00:01:36.860
"The information that [Bale was] able to get
from Bob was amazing.

00:01:36.870 --> 00:01:40.230
It was an almost surreal experience to be
able to sit down with this guy and listen

00:01:40.230 --> 00:01:41.250
to his stories.

00:01:41.250 --> 00:01:42.240
It was phenomenal."

00:01:42.740 --> 00:01:46.780
The instructors at the Bondurant High Performance
Driving School are top class, but at the end

00:01:46.780 --> 00:01:51.020
of Bale's training behind the wheel, Nagle
had to hop in a car to see the actor's progress for himself.

00:01:51.520 --> 00:01:55.820
It was during this final exam of sorts that
Bale was really put to the test, as Nagle

00:01:55.820 --> 00:01:59.590
joined Bale on the track and intentionally
placed pressure on him to see how well he

00:01:59.590 --> 00:02:01.660
could hold his composure and focus on the
road.

00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:07.220
"If you're gonna push a piece of machinery
to the limit and expect it to hold together,

00:02:07.220 --> 00:02:10.940
you have to have some sense of where that
limit is."

00:02:11.080 --> 00:02:12.040
Nagle shared:

00:02:12.040 --> 00:02:15.000
"The last day of the training, we finished
in real race cars.

00:02:15.019 --> 00:02:18.280
One of the tests that I do is to whomever
I'm training is to see how well they stay

00:02:18.280 --> 00:02:20.269
focused, to see if I can distract them.

00:02:20.269 --> 00:02:24.189
[If] they start paying more attention to my
car then they are their own car, then mistakes

00:02:24.189 --> 00:02:25.200
start to happen."

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:28.840
Nagle didn't go easy on Bale just because
he's a big-name actor, either.

00:02:28.840 --> 00:02:31.099
He admitted, quote, "I hounded him pretty
hard.

00:02:31.099 --> 00:02:35.239
I was inches off his gearbox and trying to
fill his mirrors left and right, doing everything

00:02:35.240 --> 00:02:36.780
I could to distract him."

00:02:36.780 --> 00:02:40.739
According to Nagle, Bale managed to keep his
cool and complete the task without any punch,

00:02:40.740 --> 00:02:45.000
he passed the test with flying colors and,
quote, "never bobbled once."

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:47.420
Nagle praised Bale's professionalism during
training.

00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:48.260
He admitted:

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:51.200
"He's hands down the best actor I've ever
trained."

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:53.680
Coming from a pro like Nagle, that's quite
a compliment!

00:02:54.160 --> 00:02:57.900
All of that training paid off big when it
came time to shoot Ford v Ferrari.

00:02:57.900 --> 00:03:01.440
Thanks to his extensive training, Bale knew
exactly what his car was supposed to be doing

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:06.100
during Ford v Ferrari's race scenes, allowing
him to react accordingly while "driving."

00:03:06.100 --> 00:03:09.680
Additionally, his work at the Bondurant High
Performance Driving School actually led to

00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:12.279
some real time behind the wheel on screen.

00:03:12.279 --> 00:03:13.499
As Nagle shared with Looper:

00:03:13.499 --> 00:03:16.900
"Because of the training we did with him,
I felt very comfortable putting Christian

00:03:16.900 --> 00:03:17.700
in the car."

00:03:17.700 --> 00:03:18.660
So, he did.

00:03:18.800 --> 00:03:23.239
Nagle revealed that the times in Ford v Ferrari
when you see Miles, quote, "come screaming

00:03:23.240 --> 00:03:27.060
into the pits and jump out of the car, or
jump into the car [and] merge into traffic

00:03:27.060 --> 00:03:32.400
that's going 100, 150 miles an hour" aren't
fake, and they're not the work of a stuntman, either.

00:03:33.060 --> 00:03:34.060
Nagel confessed:

00:03:34.180 --> 00:03:35.040
"That's Christian.

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:36.280
You can't fake that."

00:03:36.600 --> 00:03:40.819
Some of the cars used in the movie were remarkably
close to the real things, too, creating an

00:03:40.820 --> 00:03:42.760
even greater sense of authenticity.

00:03:42.760 --> 00:03:43.760
Nagle explained:

00:03:44.100 --> 00:03:47.560
"There's a company called Superformance, and
they're actually licensed to make what they

00:03:47.560 --> 00:03:50.279
call continuation copies of the GT40.

00:03:50.280 --> 00:03:51.560
We had a few of those."

00:03:51.560 --> 00:03:56.500
Yes, that means the cars you see in the movie
have the same engine, a Ford 427, as the one

00:03:56.500 --> 00:03:57.900
that Miles actually drove.

00:03:57.900 --> 00:04:01.400
And yes, it was just as impressive, and fun,
to handle.

00:04:01.409 --> 00:04:04.760
In Nagel's words, it was, quote, "Quite a
beast to drive."

00:04:04.760 --> 00:04:06.170
We can only imagine.

00:04:06.680 --> 00:04:08.800
"That's more like it!"

00:04:09.220 --> 00:04:11.780
Ford v Ferrari is in theaters now.

